<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig III of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludwig III was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, reigning from 1913 to 1918. Son of the prince regent Luitpold, he served as regent for his insane cousin Otto from 1912 and then had himself proclaimed king. His support for the German war effort cost him popularity, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a> proclaimed a republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in November 1918 he fled, ending more than 700 years of Wittelsbach rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-iii-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Ludwig III of Bavaria" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Ludwig,_Bavaria_LOC_29581102430.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludwig was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 7 January 1845, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luitpold-prince-regent-of-bavaria">Luitpold, Prince Regent of Bavaria</a>, and Archduchess Auguste Ferdinande of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1861 his uncle Maximilian II gave him a commission as a lieutenant, and he studied law and economics at Munich. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 he was wounded in the thigh at the Battle of Helmstadt, which contributed to his dislike of the military. On 20 February 1868 he married Archduchess Maria Theresia of Austria-Este, who brought him estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. The income allowed him to buy Leutstetten, which he expanded into one of the largest and most profitable estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He was derided as Millibauer, 'dairy farmer'. The couple had 13 children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ludwig became regent on his father's death on 12 December 1912, for his incapacitated cousin Otto. On 4 November 1913 the legislature amended the constitution so that a regency that had lasted ten years could be ended by the regent, who then assumed the crown. The next day Ludwig III was declared king. Historians regard the amendment as a definitive step toward a modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. His reign was conservative and influenced by the encyclical Rerum novarum.</p><p>He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1914 and later claimed annexations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. Accused of blind loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, he became increasingly unpopular. On 2 November 1918 a constitutional reform turned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> into a parliamentary monarchy. He fled the Residenz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> with his family on 7 November to Schloss Anif near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, the first monarch of the German Empire to be deposed, and the People's State of Bavaria was proclaimed the next day. On 12 November he issued the Anif declaration, releasing officials and soldiers from their oath to him. The word abdication never appeared, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a>'s government treated it as one, ending 738 years of Wittelsbach rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Eisner's assassination in February 1919 Ludwig fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in April 1920. He died at Sárvár in Hungary on 17 October 1921. Despite fears that a state funeral might revive the monarchy, he and his wife received one on 5 November 1921 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, with an estimated 100,000 spectators, and were buried in the crypt of the Frauenkirche.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludwig III of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His uncle Luitpold ruled as prince regent for his insane brother Otto until 1912, and Luitpold's son became King <b>Ludwig III</b> in 1913.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Sárvár Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1894" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ludwig III of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1894">What was here in 1894? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
